  "classification": {
    "primarySkillId": "solve-linear-inequalities",
    "secondarySkillIds": [],
    "reasoningScore": 74,
    "difficulty": "Mastery",
    "difficultyReason": "1E Q28: Structural selection, multiple connected steps, proof or admissible-case reasoning. The sign of 2a−1 determines the direction. Its zero case requires direct substitution; this parameter-case extension is not demonstrated in the supplied teaching examples."
  },
  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q28-parent",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Consider the inequality $(2a-1)x>3a+2$, where $a$ is a constant. State the value(s) of $a$ so the solution of $x$ is",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q28-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$x>\\text{constant}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a>\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "The sign stays unchanged when dividing by a positive coefficient.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2a-1&>0\\\\2a&>1\\\\a&>\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\n\nThen $x>\\frac{3a+2}{2a-1}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q28-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$x<\\text{constant}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a<\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "The sign reverses when dividing by a negative coefficient.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2a-1&<0\\\\2a&<1\\\\a&<\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\n\nThen $x<\\frac{3a+2}{2a-1}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q28-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "empty (no solution).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=\\frac12$.\n\nThe inequality becomes $0>\\frac72$, which is false.",
          "worked": "Every nonzero coefficient gives a nonempty half-line, so consider the zero coefficient.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}2a-1&=0\\\\a&=\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\n\nSubstitution gives $0x>3\\left(\\frac12\\right)+2$, or $0>\\frac72$. This is false for every $x$, so $a=\\frac12$ gives no solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q28-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "all real numbers.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No values of $a$.\n\nA nonzero coefficient gives a half-line; the zero coefficient gives no solution.",
          "worked": "For $a\\ne\\frac12$, division by $2a-1$ gives a strict bound on $x$.\n\nFor $a=\\frac12$, the inequality becomes $0>\\frac72$, which is false.\n\nNeither case gives all real numbers, so there are no values of $a$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      }
    ]
  },
